1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,760
Waking up with a sore jaw on nights you weren't even stressed?

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:04,963
The real cause might not be stress at all.

3
00:00:05,283 --> 00:00:10,739
Three independent triggers behind sleep bruxism are acid reflux, genetics, and ADHD medication.

4
00:00:11,059 --> 00:00:23,119
Acid reflux is one of the strongest risk factors for grinding - people with GERD are up to six times more likely to grind at night, since acid creeping up the esophagus can trigger brief awakenings that set the jaw clenching.

5
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:26,380
Genetics plays a bigger role than most people realize.

6
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:34,903
Twin studies show genetics explains roughly half the risk, and kids are about 50% more likely to grind if a parent had a history of it too.

7
00:00:35,223 --> 00:00:47,103
Stimulant medication for ADHD, like Adderall or Ritalin, is another overlooked cause - it raises dopamine activity in a way that also ramps up jaw muscle activity, often within weeks of starting.

8
00:00:47,423 --> 00:00:48,287
Frequent heartburn?

9
00:00:48,607 --> 00:00:50,877
Treating the reflux can ease the grinding too.

10
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:52,320
Family history of it?

11
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:56,355
Mention it at your next dental checkup so any wear gets caught early.

12
00:00:56,675 --> 00:01:02,828
And if grinding started after a new stimulant prescription, talk to your doctor about the dose - it's a documented side effect.
